// `Hashable` lets a managed value be used as a `Map<K, V>` key or `Set<K>`
// element, modeled on Swift's `Hashable` (which refines `Equatable`). A type
// that is `Hashable` must also be `Eq`: two values that compare equal must hash
// equal.
//
// A managed `struct` or `sum` satisfies `Hashable` by listing
// `derives(Eq, Hash)`, which the compiler expands into a structural hash and
// equality over every field — no hand-written boilerplate. The builtin hashable
// scalars (`Int`, `String`, `Bool`, ...) satisfy `Hashable` directly.
//
// `Hashable` is pure and total: it introduces no mutation, retention, native,
// or unsafe boundary, and adds no capability gate. It is the bound on
// `Map<K, V>` keys and `Set<K>` elements so that graph interning, dedup, and
// memoization over user-defined managed types are expressible while a
// non-hashable key is rejected in RSScript instead of leaking from rustc.
protocol Hashable {
    fn hash_value(self: Self) -> Int
}
